# The supply of Bratislava new-builds keeps growing, prices are standing still

{cqNameEn} of {cqYear} on the Bratislava new-build market means stronger demand, price stagnation and a further increase in the number of flats for sale. Sales reached {cqSales} flats against {pqSales} in the previous quarter. The price level was unchanged and rose by {yqM2PctEn}% year on year. The supply of flats for sale rose to {cqSupply}.

As of {asOfEn} the supply on the new-build market consisted, according to Residata's data, of {cqSupply} flats across {cqProjects} projects. Against the previous quarter supply rose by {pqSupply} flats or {pqSupplyPctEn}%, while year on year it rose by {yqSupply} flats or {yqSupplyPctEn}%.

The average price of these flats on offer reached {cqM2} €/m² incl. VAT, which year on year is an increase of only {yqM2PctEn}%. Across the whole observed period ({seriesSpan}) the average price has moved within a band best described as stagnation — a year ago the average price was {yqM2} €/m² incl. VAT and a quarter ago {pqM2} €/m² incl. VAT.

The average absolute price of flats on offer stood at {cqPrice} € incl. VAT at an average floor area of {cqAreaEn} m². The average on its own says little about any particular offer, however: one-room flats cost {r1M2} €/m² incl. VAT and three-room flats {r3M2} €/m² incl. VAT, a gap of {roomSpreadPctEn}% — more than any movement of the market over the past year. The dearest metre is in small and large flats, the cheapest in the two- and three-room middle.

