## The development of supply and sales on the Bratislava new-build market

Supply grew and demand grew with it. {cqSales} flats were sold against {pqSales} in the previous quarter and {yqSales} a year ago. Sales did not split in the proportions of supply: two-room flats are {r2ShareEn}% of the offer but {r2SoldShareEn}% of sales, while three-room are {r3ShareEn}% of the offer and only {r3SoldShareEn}% of sales. Sales were not distorted by an exceptionally high figure in one of the large projects — the strongest of them accounted for {qTopSellerN} flats. Demand therefore rose by {pqSalesPctEn}% against the previous quarter.

The average achieved price of sold flats rose slightly to {cqSoldM2} €/m² incl. VAT, matching the average asking price. Behind the rise in the price of sold flats is above all increased demand in {qD1} and {qD2}, which are dearer than the average — in {qD1} sales rose from {qD1Then} to {qD1Now} flats and in {qD2} from {qD2Then} to {qD2Now}. The average absolute price of sold flats was {cqSoldPrice} € incl. VAT at an average floor area of {cqSoldAreaEn} m².

A standing average does not mean developers leave prices alone. Over the quarter the price of {rpUnits} flats changed across {rpProjects} projects, {rpUpEn}% of the changes upward at a median of {rpStepEn}%; it touched only {rpShareEn}% of the offer, however, so it shifts the average by about {rpEffectEn}%. Working against it is new supply — {qNewUnits} flats arrived at an average of {qNewMeanM2} €/m² incl. VAT, {newBelowPctEn}% below the market, and the two forces very nearly cancel.

## A view on where the Bratislava new-build market is heading

Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata: "Over the past year sales have held at around {avgQuarterSales} flats a quarter, while supply over the same period rose by {yqSupplyPctEn}%. More flats are therefore coming onto the market than are leaving it, and the difference is accumulating.

The first factor is supply. It has reached {cqSupply} flats across {cqProjects} projects and keeps growing. A stagnant average should not lull anyone: price lists do move, they are simply held in place by cheaper supply arriving alongside.

The second factor is demand, constrained above all by interest rates and household purchasing power. And what matters here is that a city average tells a buyer nothing about what they will pay: the gap between a one-room and a three-room flat is {roomSpreadPctEn}% per metre, many times the market's own movement over the year.

Unless interest rates and purchasing power change materially, the new-build market will go on marking time, demand will stay bounded and prices will stagnate. What will be interesting to watch is the development of supply — further growth could already cause a degree of nervousness."

**Author:** Tomáš Kamhal, founder of Residata · tomas@residata.eu
